  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-53063",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191275/assets/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A parallelogram is a rectangle when all the angles are right.\n\n- Statement II is false. A kite is always a quadrilateral because it has four sides.\n\n- Statement III is false. A trapezoid has only $1$ pair of opposite sides that are parallel. However, in a square, $2$ pairs of opposite sides are parallel, and so doesn't fit the definition of a trapezoid.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
  "html": "<div id=\"questionExplanation-53063\" class=\"questionExplanation\" style=\"display: block;\"><p>First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.</p>\n<div id=\"graphic-q-53063-e-0\" class=\"graphic\"><img src=\"/graphics/q-53063-e-0\"></div>\n\n<p>Let's now look at each statement in turn.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Statement I is true. A parallelogram is a rectangle when all the angles are right.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement II is false. A kite is always a quadrilateral because it has four sides.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement III is false. A trapezoid has only <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"1\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> pair of opposite sides that are parallel. However, in a square, <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"2\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> pairs of opposite sides are parallel, and so doesn't fit the definition of a trapezoid.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In conclusion, only statement I is true.</p>\n</div>",
